[{"_1":2,"_3":4},"root",{"_5":106},"routes/item",{"_5":6},"data",{"_7":8,"_9":10},"item",{"_11":12,"_13":14,"_15":16,"_17":18,"_19":-5,"_20":21,"_22":23,"_24":25,"_26":27,"_28":27,"_29":30,"_31":32,"_33":34,"_35":36,"_37":38,"_39":40,"_41":-5,"_42":43,"_44":45,"_46":47,"_48":49,"_50":51,"_52":53,"_54":36,"_55":56,"_57":58,"_59":36,"_60":61},"expiresAt",1795394438000,"id","pkwned5oyao7mp6ha8bf6yy6w","title","USDA遭网络安全事件中断，部分农户贷款与支付服务","originalTitle","USDA Cyber Incident Disrupts Farmer Payments","summary","美国农业部（USDA）因网络安全事件关闭多项应用，导致农场服务局（FSA）贷款软件、自然资源保护局（NRCS）ProTracts支付与绘图系统及VPN出现中断。","reason","source",{"_75":105},"links",{"_61":104},"publishedAt","2026-11-22T07:50:49.000Z","discoveredAt","2026-11-22T08:04:21.988Z","timelineAt","category","activity","tags",[78,102,82,103,80,84],"score",58,"selected",false,"channel","news","story",{"_86":87,"_13":88},"x","readingMode","full","author","Deeba Ahmed","body",{"_98":-5,"_61":99,"_100":-5,"_101":36},"outline",[89,90],"relatedStories",[85],"topics",[68,69,70,71,72],"indexable","markdownAvailable",true,"group",{"_62":63,"_64":65,"_66":67},"hasTranslation","bodyLanguage","original","factId","fhr89EtippqI","reportCount",1,"additionalSourceCount",0,{"_73":83,"_75":84},{"_73":81,"_75":82},{"_73":79,"_75":80},{"_73":77,"_75":78},{"_73":74,"_75":76},"slug","blue-team","name","蓝队与运营","attack","攻击活动","vuln-mgmt","漏洞管理","incident","应急响应","cisa","CISA",{"_86":87,"_13":88},"publicId","f043cf37-b0c1-49a1-90a0-8d4fcb639348","美国农业部网络事件致支付中断",{"_11":96,"_92":97,"_94":95},{"_11":91,"_92":93,"_94":95},"sec-2","text","Food Supply Risks No Longer Limited to Data Theft","level",3,"sec-1","Farmer Loans and Payments Disrupted","zh","<p>Farmers are having trouble accessing loan and payment services after a <a href=\"https://www.nextgov.com/cybersecurity/2026/11/usda-grapples-security-breach-it-staff-take-multiple-systems-offline/417350/\">cybersecurity incident</a> forced the US Department of Agriculture (USDA) to take several applications offline this week. The disruption comes as farmers approach <a href=\"https://www.fsa.usda.gov/news-events/national-deadlines\">enrollment deadlines</a> for federal support programs, while DTN has reported delays to loans, payments and conservation work.</p><p>Who was behind the intrusion, how they infiltrated the system and the full extent of the intrusion remain unknown.</p><p>As per an internal email sent on November 16 by Chris Rottler, acting assistant chief information officer at the Farm Production and Conservation (FPAC) Business Center, the department had detected a security incident after which the staff was instructed to reset database passwords across all environments. This process also required applications to be restarted.</p><h3 id=\"sec-1\"><strong>Farmer Loans and Payments Disrupted</strong></h3><p>The outages affected many services, including Farm Service Agency (FSA) loan software, the Natural Resources Conservation Service (NRCS) ProTracts payment system and mapping software. Some employees also reported problems connecting to the department’s virtual private network (VPN).</p><p>FPAC Undersecretary Richard Fordyce <a href=\"https://www.dtnpf.com/agriculture/web/ag/columns/washington-insider/article/2026/11/19/usda-loan-software-systems-face-amid\">confirmed</a> on November 20 that it was a cyber threat and not a software update, stating that: “There was no Personally Identifiable Information (PII). No data was lost because the systems that protect against cybersecurity threats worked.”</p><p>Fordyce expected most systems to resume operations by Friday evening, with work continuing over the weekend if needed. However, reports citing two former USDA officials said hackers had accessed the farmer-payment system.</p><p>The outages have struck the department at a crucial time when farmers are dealing with deadlines to sign up for federal support programs. The Agriculture Risk Coverage (ARC) and Price Loss Coverage (PLC) <a href=\"https://www.fsa.usda.gov/news-events/news/09-15-2026/usda-announces-2026-2027-enrollment-key-price-revenue-safety-net\">programs</a> help farmers manage losses when crop prices or farm income fall, and enrollment for the 2026 programs closes on December 11. Dairy farmers have a separate deadline of December 18 to <a href=\"https://www.fsa.usda.gov/news-events/news/09-30-2026/usda-announces-2027-dairy-margin-coverage-enrollment\">enroll</a> in the 2027 Dairy Margin Coverage (DMC) program that helps protect them when milk prices fall or feed costs rise.</p><h3 id=\"sec-2\"><strong>Food Supply Risks No Longer Limited to Data Theft</strong></h3><p>A recently released Government Accountability Office (GAO) <a href=\"https://files.gao.gov/reports/GAO-27-108656/index.html\">report</a> has highlighted cybersecurity-related concerns across the food and agriculture sector. USDA and the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) had met most of 16 federal requirements, but five involving emergency preparedness and information sharing remained partly or fully unmet.</p><p>GAO’s report warned that attacks could interfere with equipment controlling irrigation, fertilizing and food production, potentially affecting food safety and public trust. GAO also <a href=\"https://www.gao.gov/blog/grocery-store-attacks-highlight-growing-food-safety-concern-cybersecurity\">identified</a> gaps in real-time threat sharing with the Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency (CISA).</p><p>“We also found that USDA and FDA don’t provide CISA with real-time information about threats. Having this information would help agencies to respond.&nbsp;As the food and agriculture sector faces increased cybersecurity risks, it is critical to ensure that the right resources are allocated to address these threats,” the agency noted.</p><p>The threat is not just about computers being unavailable, as we’ve seen in the recently reported attacks in Europe where hackers changed <a href=\"https://hackread.com/hackers-alter-refrigeration-settings-european-discard-food/\">supermarket refrigeration settings</a>, forcing stores to discard food. The USDA disruption has affected financial services for farmers, but attacks on connected agricultural equipment could reach the farms, storage facilities and food businesses too. So, the consequences of such incidents could extend well beyond an IT outage, causing much higher losses.</p><p>Security experts shared their comments with Hackread.com, saying the USDA incident highlights how cyberattacks can affect people who depend on government services, not just the systems themselves.</p><p>John Strand, Owner of Black Hills Information Security, said agriculture is often overlooked when people discuss critical infrastructure, despite its role in feeding the population.</p><p>“When people think about critical infrastructure and cyberattacks, they usually think about the power grid, water treatment facilities, dams, and things of that nature. And they’re absolutely right. But what a lot of people don’t think about is farming and ranching.”</p><p>“At the end of the day, one of the most critical functions of any country is its ability to feed its citizens. Agriculture is absolutely critical infrastructure, and we need to start treating it that way.”</p><p>Seemant Sehgal, Founder and CEO of BreachLock, focused on the password resets that caused the USDA applications to go offline. He said the incident raises questions about how well the department understands the connections between its systems.</p><p>“A blanket database password reset across every environment usually means nobody could say with confidence which credentials the intruder had touched. When one reset can take down farm loan and crop insurance payments, the dependencies between those systems were never mapped well enough to be changed safely. GAO’s findings on missed emergency exercises suggest these agencies are learning how their systems fail while they are failing, and farmers waiting on payments cover the cost of that education.”</p><p>Joshua Marpet, Senior Product Security Consultant at Finite State, pointed to the consequences of failing to meet basic cybersecurity requirements. He said these requirements are meant to make systems harder to attack, even if they cannot guarantee complete protection.</p><p>“Requirements are just that. They are the minimum requirements to not get hacked, to be at least marginally secure, to be harder to reach, and harder to break into. Not impossible, mind you. Just harder. When requirements aren’t met, then the safety margin is reduced. When people’s farm loans and personal data are at risk, reducing that safety margin seems like a bad idea.”</p><p><em>(Photo by <a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/photo/department-of-agriculture-building-exterior-36984940/\">Mark Stebnicki via Pexels</a>)</em></p>","zhKind","complete","数据泄露","加固","https://hackread.com/usda-cyber-incident-disrupts-farmer-payments/","Hackread",{"_107":108,"_109":-5},"changelogVersion","2026-10-01T08:00","release"]
